From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] the settings state belongs to the process, with no deadline that makes a second one

Patch 0007 stopped the two SettingsProvider instances the system process
constructs from each owning a copy of the settings: the second instance waits
for the first and then takes its lock and its state. The wait has a 20 s
deadline, and what it does when the deadline passes is build a separate state
"as before" -- which is the fault 0007 exists to prevent, now reached quietly
after a 20 s stall. Of the two things a late second instance can do -- keep
waiting, or own a copy of the settings nobody else reads -- the fallback picks
the one that cannot be recovered from.

There is no deadline that helps here. If the first instance has not finished
onCreate(), the only thing a second state can do is diverge from it; waiting is
the only answer that keeps the invariant. So the state stops being per-instance
at all: the five fields that onCreate() fills -- the registry, the handler
thread and its handler, the user manager and the package manager -- and the
lock that guards them belong to the class, and onCreate() builds them once,
under that lock. A second instance takes the same lock, finds them built, and
returns. If it arrives while the first is still building, it waits on the
monitor for as long as the building takes and not a second longer; if the first
instance never finishes, this one never returns, which is what a system whose
settings provider cannot start deserves and is what the Watchdog is for.

The latch, the deadline, the record of the first instance and its stack trace
all go, because with one state per class there is nothing left to coordinate.
The fields keep AOSP's names with their "m" prefix: renaming five static fields
would put this change in the 113 places that use them and bury it.

